Let me look at the `StringAppend` function once more, very carefully, for a potential heap overflow:

```c
static
void StringAppend(string* s, char c)
{
    if (s->len + 1 >= s->max)
    {
        char* new_ptr;
        s->max *= 10;
        new_ptr = AllocChunk(s->it8, s->max);
        memcpy(new_ptr, s->begin, s->len);
        s->begin = new_ptr;
    }
    s->begin[s->len++] = c;
    s->begin[s->len] = 0;
}
```

Wait, I need to think about `AllocChunk` more carefully. `AllocChunk` uses a block pool. When `StringAppend` grows, it calls `AllocChunk(s->it8, s->max)`. But `AllocChunk` aligns the size with `_cmsALIGNMEM`. So the allocated size is `_cmsALIGNMEM(s->max)`. Then `s->begin[s->len++] = c` writes at index `s->len`. Since `s->len < s->max` (because we grew when `s->len + 1 >= s->max`), and `s->max <= _cmsALIGNMEM(s->max)`, this is within bounds. So no overflow here.

